> **Foreword**
> 
> By Hon. Matia Kasaija  
> Minister of Finance, Planning and Economic Development  
> The Republic of Uganda
> 
> The President of the Conference  
> Your Excellencies, Heads of State and Government  
> Your Excellencies Heads of Delegation  
> Ladies and Gentlemen,
> 
> It is my honor to present this pivotal report, which addresses the urgent challenges posed by climate change and its profound implications for our economies. This report serves as a vital resource for Finance Ministers and economic leaders as we navigate the complexities of fiscal sustainability in a changing climate.
> 
> As we know, the impacts of climate change are increasingly evident and threaten the progress of nations, particularly those in low- and middle-income countries. This report highlights the pressing need for strategic investments in adaptation measures as a means to enhance economic resilience and stability. By recognizing the interconnectedness of climate risks and economic development, we can turn challenges into opportunities.
> 
> The findings underscore a dual imperative for all nations: first, to strengthen our analytical frameworks for assessing fiscal impacts and, second, to prioritize immediate action in adaptation investments. These investments not only safeguard our economies from potential losses but also create avenues for growth and sustainable development.
> 
> Furthermore, we must align our adaptation strategies with broader economic policies and frameworks, ensuring that they are informed by scientific insights and grounded in the realities of our unique contexts. Only through such comprehensive planning can we leverage the full benefits of adaptation and mobilize the required resources to drive effective change.
> 
> It is essential that we foster collaboration within our governments and across international borders. The cooperative efforts among nations, stakeholders, and institutions will be crucial in addressing the financial challenges posed by climate change. 
> 
> In closing, the economic rationale for adaptation is clear—acting now will not only mitigate the risks associated with climate change but will also build resilient economies for the future. I invite all finance leaders to engage with the insights presented in this report, ensuring that our strategies are both adaptive and resourceful.
> 
> Thank you.
